1
00:00:01,284 --> 00:00:04,241
On 26 December 2004,

2
00:00:04,241 --> 00:00:06,753
a magnitude 9.1 earthquake broke

3
00:00:06,753 --> 00:00:08,334
the seafloor off Sumatra.

4
00:00:09,684 --> 00:00:11,379
The tsunami took 2 hours

5
00:00:11,379 --> 00:00:13,362
36 minutes to reach Chennai,

6
00:00:13,362 --> 00:00:15,568
and almost three hours to reach Colombo.

7
00:00:16,279 --> 00:00:18,815
The Indian Ocean had no warning system.

8
00:00:18,815 --> 00:00:21,791
More than 227,000 people died.

9
00:00:23,116 --> 00:00:24,605
This is LeadTime.

10
00:00:24,605 --> 00:00:26,264
It asks one question:

11
00:00:26,264 --> 00:00:28,638
how many minutes does a coastal city get to run?

12
00:00:29,572 --> 00:00:31,267
Replay 2004,

13
00:00:31,267 --> 00:00:33,178
and a shallow-water solver carries the

14
00:00:33,178 --> 00:00:35,341
wave across the real ocean floor,

15
00:00:35,341 --> 00:00:36,334
live in your browser.

16
00:00:36,967 --> 00:00:39,840
Over deep water it moves as fast as a jet,

17
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:41,607
and twelve hours of tsunami play

18
00:00:41,607 --> 00:00:42,839
out in under a minute.

19
00:00:43,763 --> 00:00:46,047
The seafloor bends it into beams.

20
00:00:46,047 --> 00:00:49,214
Here's where it hit hardest, and when, hour by hour.

21
00:00:50,153 --> 00:00:51,980
For 65 cities on the coast,

22
00:00:51,980 --> 00:00:53,398
you see when the wave arrives,

23
00:00:53,398 --> 00:00:55,208
and how many people live there.

24
00:00:55,822 --> 00:00:58,822
With no sensors, nobody gets a confirmed warning.

25
00:00:59,734 --> 00:01:01,249
Now you're the engineer.

26
00:01:01,249 --> 00:01:03,893
Drop one deep-ocean sensor west of Sumatra,

27
00:01:03,893 --> 00:01:06,489
and 73.1 million people get at

28
00:01:06,489 --> 00:01:08,323
least fifteen minutes to evacuate.

29
00:01:08,979 --> 00:01:13,157
Chennai, with 6.7 million people, gets 101 minutes.

30
00:01:14,122 --> 00:01:16,286
The optimizer tests 15 possible

31
00:01:16,286 --> 00:01:18,004
earthquakes along the trenches,

32
00:01:18,004 --> 00:01:19,598
and places six sensors.

33
00:01:20,246 --> 00:01:23,600
The first warns about 53 million people per quake.

34
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:25,157
The sixth adds nothing.

35
00:01:25,816 --> 00:01:28,424
Real networks also weigh cost and upkeep,

36
00:01:28,424 --> 00:01:30,546
but now you can see what each sensor buys.

37
00:01:31,596 --> 00:01:33,014
And the honest part:

38
00:01:33,014 --> 00:01:35,791
Banda Aceh is hit in 21 minutes.

39
00:01:35,791 --> 00:01:37,312
No sensor can save it.

40
00:01:38,017 --> 00:01:41,238
On nearby Simeulue Island, an oral tradition did.

41
00:01:41,238 --> 00:01:44,514
Only seven of more than 78,000 people died there.

42
00:01:45,539 --> 00:01:47,186
Can you trust the physics?

43
00:01:47,186 --> 00:01:50,443
Against 13 tide gauges from 2004,

44
00:01:50,443 --> 00:01:53,207
our arrival times are off by 20 minutes on average,

45
00:01:53,207 --> 00:01:54,487
and mostly early.

46
00:01:55,184 --> 00:01:57,727
So the warning times you see err on the safe side.

47
00:01:58,947 --> 00:02:00,095
LeadTime.

48
00:02:00,095 --> 00:02:03,003
Free, open source, no login.

49
00:02:03,003 --> 00:02:04,243
Every minute counts.
